-- What a string literal costs once nothing has to test where its bytes live.
--
-- A literal's run is `runBorrowed` - `stringLiteral` writes the constant, `Native/Run.native.yana`'s
-- `releaseRun` tests it, and the test is the whole of `Reclaim(String)`. So the teardown is provably
-- no instructions at all, and for a long time it was emitted anyway: the constant was written into a
-- `Run`, copied into an `Array`, copied into a `StringData`, **moved** into the caller's `String`
-- slot, and read back through a `borrow` and a `cast`. Four of those five steps were places
-- `opt_place.cpp` stopped carrying what it knew - see Analysis-Borrows.md §8.9.
--
-- `.opt.expect` is what this asserts and it is the point of the fixture: `length(s)` folds to the
-- literal's own count, and after the construction there is **no borrow, no cast, no load, no
-- placement test, no branch and no `freeHeap`**. An array literal has always optimized this way; the
-- string is the shape that could not reach it.
--
-- Native only. On JS a literal is a host string and the host collector owns the bytes, so there is
-- no placement flag for any of this to be about.

fn litLen(n: Size) -> Size:
    let s = "hello"
    return length(s) + n

-- The same one level out, so that what is asserted is the fold rather than one function's shape.
fn twice(n: Size) -> Size = litLen(n) + litLen(n + 1)

fn main() -> Int = truncate(twice(3)) :: Int
